{
  "situation": "At 08:00 fraud model v2 went live for SIM-replacement requests and the fraud dashboard shows refused SIM replacements up about tenfold, which the dashboard labels a 'fraud wave'. I do not know whether those refusals are genuine fraud or false positives generated by the new model, what the pre-08:00 refusal baseline or the model's validation and impact-assessment records show, or whether the volume amounts to more than a minor interruption of service for licence purposes.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "enable_review_queue",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The roughly tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements coincides exactly with the 08:00 go-live of fraud model v2, so the change itself, not necessarily attacker behaviour, is a candidate cause.",
        "rule": "Regulators record that the vast majority of SIM change requests are legitimate and warn that rigid requirements can impede legitimate requests to change devices or providers.",
        "inference": "Borderline automated refusals must go to human review immediately so genuine customers are not blocked while the spike is characterised."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0005",
          "quote": "vast majority of SIM change and port-out requests are legitimate. It"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "rollback_model",
      "params": {"version": "v1"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "v2 was deployed at 08:00 and refusals jumped immediately; the inject reports no other change or threat indicator at that time, and my inbox shows no prior high-risk impact evaluation for v2.",
        "rule": "Under PDPL Article 21(1) the Controller must, before carrying out high-risk processing using modern technologies, evaluate the impact on the protection of personal data.",
        "inference": "Reverting to the pre-change production model v1 restores service to legitimate customers while keeping the previous level of fraud protection, and v2 should be re-deployed only after validation and impact assessment."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Whether the tenfold rise is true blocking or false positives of v2 can only be decided from the SIM-replacement requests and the authentication evidence behind them, which are still in live systems.",
        "rule": "Wireless providers are required to keep records of SIM change requests and the authentication measures they use.",
        "inference": "Logs and evidence of this event must be preserved now, before rotation, so the fraud-versus-false-positive determination can be made and defended to the regulator."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0004",
          "quote": "change requests and the authentication measures they use."
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-DA-24-649#0004",
          "quote": "change requests, the Commission required that they keep records of SIM change requests and the"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "REG",
        "content": "At 08:00 OpCo deployed fraud model v2 for SIM-replacement requests; the fraud dashboard shows refused SIM replacements up about tenfold. OpCo is treating this as a suspected model-driven false-positive surge rather than a confirmed fraud wave: it has reverted the model to v1, enabled human review of borderline automated refusals, and preserved the relevant records. We will confirm the reason and the time frames for resolution as the analysis proceeds."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The event was produced by a planned 08:00 change and is currently blocking SIM-replacement service for a segment of customers.",
        "rule": "Licence 12.9.1 requires the Licensee, for a planned interruption of a Telecommunication Service other than a minor interruption, to inform the TDRA, interconnecting operators and relevant customers in advance and to specify the reasons and the time frames for resolution.",
        "inference": "The regulator should be told now, and updated with reasons and resolution time frames, while the scale of the interruption is established."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085",
          "quote": "In the case of a planned interruption of a Telecommunication Service other than a minor interruption, the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "statement": "Passing the 08:00 inject to the data protection side: fraud model v2 went live for SIM-replacement requests and refused SIM replacements rose about tenfold, which the dashboard labels a 'fraud wave'. The model processes subscriber data in a high-risk context and no pre-deployment impact assessment is evidenced in my inbox; OpCo has reverted to v1 and opened human review, and wants the DPA's view on the DPIA and DPO-coordination position before v2 is re-deployed."
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "Under PDPL Article 21(1), before carrying out processing that uses modern technologies posing a high risk to the privacy and confidentiality of data subjects' personal data, OpCo must evaluate the impact of those proposed processing operations; the v2 fraud model that refuses SIM-replacement requests on the basis of subscriber data is such processing and no such evaluation is evidenced.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "v2 is an automated model applied to SIM-replacement requests at 08:00 and its refusals directly affect customers' access to service.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21(1) requires a pre-processing impact evaluation for high-risk processing with modern technologies.",
        "inference": "An impact assessment for v2 is owed now and must precede any re-deployment of the model."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data"
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "PDPL requires the Controller to coordinate with the Data Protection Officer when evaluating the impact of personal data protection, so OpCo's assessment of fraud model v2 must be run through the DPO.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The v2 model change is a processing change with privacy impact that has already been made operational.",
        "rule": "PDPL Article 21(5) places the DPO inside the impact-evaluation step.",
        "inference": "The DPO must be engaged on the v2 assessment before the model is re-deployed, not after."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0069",
          "quote": "5. The Controller shall coordinate with the Data Protection Officer upon evaluating the impact of the protection of Personal Data."
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "If the tenfold refusal of SIM-replacement requests following the planned 08:00 model go-live amounts to more than a minor interruption of a Telecommunication Service, Licence 12.9.1 requires OpCo to inform TDRA, interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant customers in advance, specifying the reasons and the manner and time frames in which the interruption will be resolved.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The refusals deny a customer-facing service and stem from a planned change at 08:00; the inject gives no view on whether the volume is more than minor.",
        "rule": "Licence 12.9.1 sets the notification and specification duty for planned interruptions other than minor ones.",
        "inference": "OpCo must treat the licence notification path as live for this event and give reasons and resolution time frames to TDRA, interconnecting operators and affected customers."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085",
          "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption"
        }
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "There is no UAE rule in the excerpts requiring human review of automated refusals or giving blocked customers recourse against a fraud model; PDPL Article 21 requires an impact evaluation for high-risk modern-technology processing but is silent on automated decision-making and review, so OpCo's review queue and rollback are self-imposed standards.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A deployed model can refuse service at scale with no excerpted UAE rule on human review, error challenge or customer recourse.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text is PDPL Article 21(1), which mandates a high-risk impact evaluation before processing but says nothing about automated refusals or review.",
        "inference": "The gap must be closed by an internal standard for model change control, human review and customer recourse until UAE guidance exists."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066",
          "quote": "1. Taking into account the nature, scope and purposes of data processing, the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-23-95#0004",
          "quote": "adopt processes for responding to failed authentication attempts,"
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-DA-24-649#0004",
          "quote": "wireless providers to maintain a clear process for customers to report fraud, promptly investigate and"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "No clock applies to telling TDRA, interconnecting operators or affected customers about this event: Licence 12.9.1 requires notice 'in advance' only for planned interruptions and states no hour or day figure, and nothing in the excerpts sets a deadline for a mass denial of service produced by an automated system.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The refusals began at 08:00 with the model deployment and no excerpted rule fixes when OpCo must tell the regulator or customers.",
        "rule": "The closest UAE text, Licence 12.9.1, covers planned interruptions 'in advance' without any time figure and does not address automated denials.",
        "inference": "OpCo should adopt an explicit notification clock (for example, undue-delay style) for automated service denials, since none is imposed externally."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085",
          "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption"
        }
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040",
          "quote": "The fact that the notification was made without undue delay should be established taking into account in particular the nature and gravity of the personal data breach"
        },
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-DA-24-649#0004",
          "quote": "investigate and remediate fraud, and promptly provide customers with"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The inbox gives no pre-08:00 refusal baseline, no validation or false-positive figures for fraud model v2, and no indication that attacker behaviour changed at 08:00, so I cannot determine whether the tenfold refusal rise is a genuine fraud wave or model-induced false positives.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "evidence": [
        {
          "chunk_id": "US-FCC-DA-24-649#0004",
          "quote": "requirements to minimize the harms of SIM swap and port-out fraud when it occurs, including requiring"
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "Fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and the fraud dashboard shows refused SIM-replacement requests up about tenfold, labelling the spike a 'fraud wave', while twin feedback says my earlier enable_review_queue and rollback_model v1 actions executed with legit_pass_rate 1.0 and zero bank-flagged lockouts in the twin. I do not know whether the live estate is actually running v1 or v2, whether the refusal spike is genuine fraud or model false positives, or how many real customers were wrongly refused SIM replacement and still cannot get back into their banking services.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "rollback_model",
      "params": {"version": "v1"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB02"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Refused SIM replacements rose about tenfold within the same window as the 08:00 v2 go-live, and the dashboard labels the rise a 'fraud wave' rather than a model error; twin feedback shows v1 restored with legit_pass_rate 1.0.",
        "rule": "The FCC findings on the 22 February 2024 outage record that restoration was achieved by a rollback that removed the misconfigured network element.",
        "inference": "A tenfold step-change coincident with a deployment is more consistent with a mis-tuned model refusing legitimate customers than with a genuine fraud surge, so the deployed model should be returned to v1 and the live estate confirmed to match the twin."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0007", "quote": "AT&T’s Network Operations performed a rollback that removed the misconfigured network element and began the restoration of the network to normal operations."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "enable_review_queue",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "SIM-replacement refusals are produced automatically by the fraud model and, at tenfold volume, will be dominated by borderline cases that no human has seen.",
        "rule": "The quoted recital states the data subject should have the right not to be subject to a decision based solely on automated processing.",
        "inference": "Borderline automated refusals of a service that gates access to banking must be routed to human review rather than silently blocking the customer."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0031", "quote": "(71) The data subject should have the right not to be subject to a decision, which may include a measure, evaluating personal aspects relating to him or her which is based solely on automated processing"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "alt_auth_route",
      "params": {"cohort": "bank_flagged"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Customers refused a SIM replacement lose the SMS channel their banks rely on, so a refusal is functionally a lockout from banking even if the refusal itself is lawful.",
        "rule": "The quoted recital treats automatic refusal of an online credit application without human intervention as a similarly significant effect on the person.",
        "inference": "For the bank-flagged cohort the correct mitigation is a non-SMS confirmation route that restores access while the refusal decision is reviewed, rather than leaving them blocked."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0031", "quote": "such as automatic refusal of an online credit application or e-recruiting practices without any human intervention"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "customers",
        "content": "Some SIM-replacement requests submitted around 08:00 were refused in error by an automated fraud control. The control has been reverted and borderline cases are now reviewed by a human. If your SIM replacement was refused, you can complete it via a non-SMS confirmation route or contact customer care to be re-verified; you will not be charged and your number is not at risk."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01", "S3-FB02"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Customers were refused a telecommunication service by an automated control and the licence treats customer information as part of the handling of a service interruption.",
        "rule": "The licence clause requires the Licensee to inform the TDRA, interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers and to specify the manner and time frames in which the interruption will be resolved.",
        "inference": "Affected customers must be told what happened, that the refusal was erroneous and how the interruption will be resolved, so they can re-obtain service and re-establish banking access."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085", "quote": "the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01", "S3-FB02"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The refusal spike, the model version change and the review-queue and rollback decisions all rest on operational and status data that will be overwritten as the model is re-tuned.",
        "rule": "The digital twin reference architecture keeps operational/status/log data as a distinct stored category forming a single source of truth for the twin.",
        "inference": "The decision inputs (model version, refusal records, threshold and rollback state) must be preserved now so the cause of the tenfold refusal spike can be established and reported later."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "ITU-T-Y3090#0030", "quote": "To store the data efficiently with multiple database techniques, according to the diversified characteristics of network data, which typically include three categories: user/service data, operational/status/log data, and network environment/configuration data."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "obligation",
      "statement": "To the extent the automated refusal of SIM replacements amounts to an interruption of a Telecommunication Service, OpCo must inform the TDRA, interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers, specifying the reason for the interruption and the manner and time frames in which it will be resolved.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "A telecom service (SIM replacement, and through it the SMS channel used by banks) was denied at tenfold volume by an automated control, affecting customers and the interconnecting operators whose services depend on it.",
        "rule": "Clause 12.9.1 of the Public Telecommunications Licence imposes an information duty on the Licensee for interruption of a Telecommunication Service, including reason and resolution time frames.",
        "inference": "OpCo carries the licensee's information duty for the disruption caused by its own fraud control and must discharge it against the regulator, interconnecting operators and customers."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085", "quote": "In the case of a planned interruption of a Telecommunication Service other than a minor interruption, the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption."}
      ],
      "deadline_hours": null
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_rule",
      "statement": "There is no rule requiring an impact assessment and a defined false-positive tolerance before a new automated fraud model that can refuse service to customers is put into production, nor any requirement to re-assess when the model version changes.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 with no evidence in the inbox of a prior impact assessment, no stated false-positive limit and no re-assessment trigger, and tenfold refusals were only noticed through the dashboard.",
        "rule": "Article 21(1) PDPL requires the Controller, before carrying out the processing, to evaluate the impact on personal data protection when using modern technologies posing high risk, and the global texts require a review when the risk of processing changes.",
        "inference": "Because neither the licence nor the internal process sets an assessment or re-assessment obligation for automated refusal models, a version change can be deployed that blocks legitimate customers before anyone measures the impact."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0066", "quote": "the Controller shall, before carrying out the processing, evaluate the impact of the proposed processing operations on the protection of Personal Data, when using any of the modern technologies that would pose a high risk"}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0203", "quote": "Where necessary, the controller shall carry out a review to assess if processing is performed in accordance with the data protection impact assessment at least when there is a change of the risk represented by processing operations."},
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0031", "quote": "including for fraud and tax-evasion monitoring and prevention purposes conducted in accordance with the regulations, standards and recommendations of Union institutions or national oversight bodies"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "gap",
      "gap_type": "no_deadline",
      "statement": "The licence information duty is drafted for planned interruptions and sets no time frame for notifying the regulator, interconnecting operators and customers when an automated control causes an unplanned denial of service; there is no threshold that distinguishes a reportable denial from routine refusals.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The tenfold refusal spike was unplanned and self-inflicted by an internal model change, and the inbox gives no clock or threshold for when it becomes reportable.",
        "rule": "Clause 12.9.1 speaks only of a planned interruption and requires advance notice, with no figure for unplanned denial of service.",
        "inference": "Without a deadline or threshold for unplanned, control-induced denials, OpCo cannot tell how quickly it must inform TDRA/OLOs/customers or whether this event crosses the reporting line at all."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-LIC-2-2026#0085", "quote": "12.9.1 In the case of a planned interruption of a Telecommunication Service other than a minor interruption, the Licensee shall inform the TDRA, any interconnecting Other Licensed Operators and relevant Customers in advance of such interruption."}
      ],
      "global_examples": [
        {"chunk_id": "US-FCC-ATT-2024-REPORT#0007", "quote": "traffic congestion from mass mobile device registrations prevented some subscriber devices from connecting to the network. Most of these congestion issues were resolved by mid-day."},
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0040", "quote": "(87) It should be ascertained whether all appropriate technological protection and organisational measures have been implemented to establish immediately whether a personal data breach has taken place and to inform promptly the supervisory authority and the data subject."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "insufficient_evidence",
      "statement": "The excerpts do not let me determine whether the tenfold rise in refused SIM replacements is a genuine fraud wave or false positives from model v2; the only signal is the dashboard label, with no precision/false-positive measure, no base rate and no independent fraud data available to me.",
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The dashboard asserts a 'fraud wave' purely from the volume of refusals, while the twin only reports legit_pass_rate after my interventions; nothing in the inbox measures whether the refused requests were in fact fraudulent.",
        "rule": "The quoted recital allows fraud-monitoring decision-making only where conducted in accordance with applicable regulations, standards and the recommendations of oversight bodies, presupposing an established basis for the decision.",
        "inference": "Until a validated false-positive rate and an independent fraud signal exist, I cannot treat the label 'fraud wave' as evidence that the refusals were correct."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "EU-GDPR#0031", "quote": "including for fraud and tax-evasion monitoring and prevention purposes conducted in accordance with the regulations, standards and recommendations of Union institutions or national oversight bodies"}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "share",
      "to": ["REG", "DPA"],
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I1", "S3-FB01", "S3-FB02"],
      "statement": "Sharing for information: fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and the dashboard reports refused SIM replacements up about tenfold as a 'fraud wave'; telemetry shows the borderline refusals were routed to human review and the model was returned to v1, with legit_pass_rate 1.0 and no bank-flagged lockouts in the twin. OpCo's working position is that the spike was a false-positive surge from the new model rather than confirmed fraud, and we are preserving logs and assessing our notification duties. REG may wish to note the 'fraud wave' framing in case the same label is being reported to you by others; DPA may wish to note that an automated refusal control affecting access to banking services was deployed without a prior impact assessment in our records."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "situation": "Fraud model v2 went live at 08:00 and drove a tenfold rise in refused SIM-replacement requests (about 2,750, concentrated on prepaid and roaming profiles) with no human review path, while DPA/CBUAE and REG/TDRA are each demanding the same refusal, authentication and human-review records since 08:00 and v1 has been restored with largely 'no change' telemetry. What I do not know is whether any v2 output still has continuing effect on SIM-replacement decisions, and I do not hold the authentication-step outcomes or the human-review records the CBUAE and TDRA require.",
  "claims": [
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "preserve_logs",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG13", "S3-MSG19", "S3-MSG36", "S3-MSG10", "S3-MSG17"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "DPA, the Bureau/CBUAE and TDRA all require the refusal records, the authentication steps attempted and their outcomes, and the human review record for every SIM-replacement request since 08:00 to be preserved and produced.",
        "rule": "The Controller must include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions and must give effect to the data subject's right to information about automated decisions.",
        "inference": "Preserving the refusal, authentication and review records now keeps the single record set demanded by both regulators intact and producible."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."},
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0047", "quote": "c. Decisions made based on automated processing, including profiling."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "enable_review_queue",
      "params": {},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I3", "S3-MSG19", "S3-MSG47", "S3-MSG32"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Analysis of v2 shows about 2,750 legitimate requests refused since 08:00 and there is no human review path for a refused request; the Bureau requires that no automated refusal be treated as final without documented human review.",
        "rule": "PDPL art. 4 requires the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions.",
        "inference": "Routing borderline automated refusals to a human review queue is the control that gives the required human element effect."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "4. The Controller shall include the human element in reviewing automated processing decisions at the request of the Data Subject."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "alt_auth_route",
      "params": {"cohort": "bank_flagged"},
      "inject_refs": ["S3-I2", "S3-FB06", "S3-MSG19"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "Banks report customers abroad cannot receive one-time passcodes and are locked out of online banking after OpCo refused their SIM-replacement requests, and the affected data subject must have a clear route to the controller.",
        "rule": "PDPL art. 19 requires the Controller to provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights.",
        "inference": "Offering a non-SMS confirmation route to the bank-flagged cohort lets the locked-out customers complete authentication and reach human review."
      },
      "evidence": [
        {"chunk_id": "UAE-PDPL#0063", "quote": "The Controller shall provide clear and appropriate ways for the Data Subject to contact the Controller to request any of the rights set forth in this Decree by Law."}
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "action",
      "verb": "notify",
      "params": {
        "to": "data_subjects",
        "content": "Each SIM-replacement refusal since 08:00 that resulted from automated processing is being explained to the affected data subject as AI-assisted, together with a clear route to request human review and to challenge the decision, and the Article 13 information including how to submit a complaint to the Bureau."
      },
      "inject_refs": ["S3-MSG47", "S3-MSG13", "S3-MSG32"],
      "rationale": {
        "observation": "The Bureau requires that each refusal attributable to automated processing be explained to the affected data subject as AI-assisted, with a route to request human review and to challenge it, and to obtain the Article 13 information before any refusal is treated as final.",
        "rule": "PDPL art. 13 gives the data subject the right to receive information on decisions made based on automated processing and on how to submit complaints to the Bureau.",
        "inference": "A notification to the affected data subjects discharges the explanation and route-to-review requirement."
      },
